## =====================================================================================
##   MLR1 linear in parameters
##   MLR2 random sampling
##   MLR3 no PERFECT collinearity. FAILS when:
##        - one x is an exact linear function of others: sq ft and sq m; income in $ and in $1000s;
##          totexpend = expendA + expendB; male + female + intercept; age and birth year (same year)
##        - n < K + 1  (5 x's on 4 observations).  4 x's on 5 observations is OK.
##        - (simple regression) x never varies: everyone has educ = 12 -> SLR3 fails (PS9 #10)
##        Highly but NOT perfectly correlated x's (0.95): MLR3 HOLDS, but Var(bj) is large.
##        In R, a perfectly collinear x shows coefficient NA (PS11 #8)
v <- vote1; v$totexpend <- v$expendA + v$expendB
coef(lm(voteA ~ expendA + expendB + totexpend, data = v))   # totexpend -> NA
##   MLR4 E[U | x's] = 0. FAILS (x endogenous) when a variable that affects y AND is correlated
##        with an x is left in U, or the functional form is wrong.
##   MLR5 Var(U | x's) = sigma^2 (homoskedasticity). FAILS when the spread of y grows with x
##        (food spending vs income, PS9 #17). OLS STILL UNBIASED; only the variance formula breaks.
##   MLR1-MLR4 -> OLS UNBIASED: averaged over many random samples, bj-hat equals beta_j.
##        (one sample still misses; estimates differ sample to sample; residuals are not 0)
##   MLR1-MLR5 -> GAUSS-MARKOV: OLS is BLUE. "Best" = SMALLEST VARIANCE among linear unbiased estimators.
##   Why not add every variable? It can raise the variance and can break MLR3 (PS11 #10).
##   Population quantity (mu_Y, beta1) = a fixed number. Sample statistic (Ybar, b1-hat) = a random variable.
##   bj in MLR = the change in y-hat from +1 in xj HOLDING THE OTHER x's FIXED (ceteris paribus).


